 of  subgroup of poet patients so this is  approximately eight covet patients  um  who are actually  very similar to normal patients at the  level of gene expression  and on the other hand there's the  majority of the covariate patients about  three-fourths of these covariate  patients  look very very distinct from uh normal  patients  so  what could be the reason for this  uh is something that we try to figure  out in uh in this paper  so we had some  clinical annotation for these patients  like whether they had some  cardiovascular complications whether  they had diabetes they had liver  liver complications or other  comorbidities  and we did not find any signature so  basically these comorbidities could not  be the reason why the patients were  separated so here we have colored  uh a no is colored in red and a yes is  colored in in this blue color  right so what we find is that it's you  cannot clearly see that okay one of the  comorbidities like diabetes  is more enriched in the patients which  are  similar to the normal patients  versus patients which are extremely  distinct  although we do know that patients with  cardiovascular complications and  diabetes from our own work we have shown  in indian patients that  patients who have diabetes are more  susceptible to covert 19 but that's at  the clinical outcome level but at least  at the molecular level of how their  lungs are looking uh the gene expression  of their lungs are looking that's not  the case  so then what do we do we essentially go  ahead and assume  uh that these  two groups of patients are distinct and  then we compare each of these groups  with the normal sets of patients so  normal sets of patients here would be  these patients in red so what we do is  we do a comparison of differential gene  expression between this group which is  similar to normal which we call g1  and this other group which is very  distinct from normal that we call g2  when we do this  we find  as expected lots of genes tha